```python
def walk_rows(obj):
    found = {}
    if isinstance(obj, dict):
        if "scheme" in obj and isinstance(obj.get("rows"), list):
            for r in obj["rows"]:
                if isinstance(r, dict) and "dr" in r and "l2_error" in r:
                    found.setdefault(obj["scheme"], []).append((r["dr"], r["l2_error"]))
        for v in obj.values():
            for s, rows in walk_rows(v).items():
                found.setdefault(s, []).extend(rows)
    elif isinstance(obj, list):
        for v in obj:
            for s, rows in walk_rows(v).items():
                found.setdefault(s, []).extend(rows)
    return found
```

This will find the studies list entries. Note double counting risk: the dict has "rows" but I only take from the entry with "scheme", and then recurse into v including "rows" — but rows entries don't have "scheme" so no double count. Good.

